Hours Worked: 10.5 hours

Work Completed:
Developed the structural and density analysis framework for the MW–M31 merger pipeline, starting with a Claude generated scaffold and then establishing the configuration, data ingestion, and vectorized computation infrastructure for large-scale spatial diagnostics. Implemented automated snapshot extraction, loading, and center-of-mass alignment routines to combine Milky Way and Andromeda particle distributions into a unified reference frame. Configured simulation parameters, radial binning schemes, projected map geometries, physical constants, visualization settings, and memory pre-allocation to support efficient processing of the full merger simulation.

Designed and implemented optimized algorithms for computing three-dimensional density profiles, projected surface density profiles, logarithmic density slopes, progenitor-specific density distributions, and spatial mixing diagnostics using vectorized histogram-based techniques. Developed routines for calculating continuous half-mass radii, central density estimates, inner and outer density slope measurements, and projected density maps while incorporating particle-count thresholds, numerical interpolation, Gaussian smoothing, and robust handling of sparse regions. Integrated these calculations into a synchronized processing pipeline capable of iterating through all simulation snapshots while recording structural evolution and diagnostic telemetry.

Produced the first suite of structural visualization tools for the merger analysis, including multi-epoch radial density profile comparisons, logarithmic density slope heatmaps, temporal evolution of central density and half-mass radii, and large-scale projected density map grids for the combined system and individual progenitor galaxies. Refined plotting layouts, logarithmic scaling, interpolation methods, color normalization, annotation, and automated output generation to provide consistent, publication-quality figures documenting the evolution of the merger's spatial structure and mass distribution.
